ne applications with the US  central one can connect to that  particular instance once you choose a  region you can either choose a  particular zone  we're the master instance we'll be  deployed or you can choose any if you  choose any we automatically pick the  zone for the master and the replica  instance the next thing you want to  select is instance capacity or the size  of the Redis instance memory source  allows you to choose anywhere between 1  and 300 gigabytes and depending on the  size as you can see the instance will  provide different network throughput for  the 300 gigabytes you can see that  you'll get about 1,500 megabytes per  second and for one gig one gigabyte you  get about 375 megabytes per second once  you've selected the right size for your  application the next thing you need to  select is the network or the VPC network  where the instance is going to get  deployed the VPC network determines  which VMS can connect to the instance  once you've selected the network we can  also choose the eviction policy if you  choose to change it from the default in  this case I'm going to select all keys  LRU and then you go to and click create  once you click create you can see that  the instance is getting provisioned but  before I tell you how to connect to the  instance I just want to quickly touch  upon how easy it is to scale a deployed  instance now if you want to change the  size of a deployed instance you click on  the instance and click on the edit  button once you go into the Edit Page  all you need to do is change the size of  the instance if you want to change one  GB to 5 GB just change the size and  click Save and we automatically scaled  instance for you one thing to note here  is that the basic tier when you scale a  basic tier of tier instance we fully  flush the cache and also the application  gets disconnected but in standard tier  the scaling is mostly at all an  operation where you'll lose connectivity  for a brief period and there may be a  little bit of data loss because of  unreplicated data so as you can see it  is very easy to create and scale a Redis  instance using memory store now let's  take a look at how we can connect to the  memory store instance as you can see  when we deploy the memories for instance  we deploy using a  private IP address this is great because  the your reticent sense is automatically  protected from the Internet  because it can only be connected using a  private IP cloud memory server is also  fully read as protocol compatible so you  can actually progress benchmark against  it and let's see how it goes as you can  see when you run the benchmark you get  pretty good throughput with the cloud  memory store instance that you've  deployed so the next obvious question is  how do I monitor the instance to monitor  the instance there are different  multiple ways to do it if you go into  the details of an instance in cloud  console there are a few metrics that we  have published that you can use to  monitor the instance but we have more  detailed monitoring available in  stackdriver  and with the stack driver integration a  lot more of the metrics are available  through the stack driver dashboard as  you can see if you go into stack driver  you can see all the key metrics that you  need to monitor a Redis instance as you  can see average commands per second the  95th percentile latency cash at ratio  etc are all available and these are  important metrics that you need to  determine how your instances functioning  if you want to see the full list of all  the metrics that are available just go  to metrics Explorer type in Redis and  you can see the full list of metrics  that are published in stackdriver  so as you can see with cloud memory  store creating managing and monitoring  Redis instance is actually very easy  so in short with cloud memory store you  don't have to worry about maintaining  your own VM to manage your reticence  instead you can simply click a few  buttons and then create manage and  monitor the instance  so in summary let's take a look at a  quick look at the offering with cloud  memory store you have a basic tier and a  standard tier and within the tiers you  also have memory tiers which gives you  different memory sizes with different  network performance as you can see the  m1 capacity tier gives you 3 gigabits  per second  m5 gives you about 12 gigabits per  second also with respect to pricing you  get a wide range of pricing options that  allows you to choose the right tier for  you so as you can see basic m1 is less  than five cents per gigabyte hour and  the standard instances are a little bit  more basic tier is ideal if you want to  have a low cost simple right as cash  [Music]
 

